2. Preliminaries

2.1 External Environment and Internal State

The external world is a Markov decision process (MDP) with state space  \mathcal{X} , action space  \mathcal{A} , transition kernel  P^e(x' \mid x,a) , reward function  R^e(x,a) , and discount factor  \gamma \in [0,1) . The agent possesses an internal cognitive state  s \in \mathcal{S} , a Polish space, which encodes all possessed information: belief about the external state, retrieved memories, reasoning context, etc. The mapping from histories to internal states is determined by the sequence of cognitive operations applied.

2.2 Cognitive Operations as Temporal Abstractions

A primitive cognitive operation  o \in \mathcal{O}_0  is a tuple  (T_o, c, \tau) , where
 T_o(s' \mid s)  is a Markov kernel on  \mathcal{S} ,
 c(s,o) \ge 0  is a non‑negative computational cost (in units commensurable with reward), and
 \tau(s,o) \ge 1  is a strictly positive integer duration (in discrete time steps).
Applying operation  o  in state  s  produces a new internal state  s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s)  after  \tau(s,o)  steps. The set  \mathcal{O}_0  of primitive operations is finite, with  |\mathcal{O}_0| = n . The agent may also choose a special stop action  \bot  to terminate internal computation and execute an external action; stopping incurs zero duration and zero cost.

Composition. From primitives we can form composed operations by sequencing. For a finite sequence  \mathbf{o} = (o_1, \dots, o_k) , the composed operation  \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})  is defined by concatenating transition kernels and accumulating costs and durations:

T_{\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})}(s_k \mid s_0) = \int \cdots \int \prod_{i=1}^k T_{o_i}(s_i \mid s_{i-1})\, ds_1 \cdots ds_{k-1},

\tau(s_0, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\bigl[ \tau(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s_0 \bigr], \qquad
c(s_0, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\bigl[ c(S_{i-1}, o_i) \mid s_0 \bigr],

where  S_0 = s_0  and  S_i \sim T_{o_i}(\cdot \mid S_{i-1}) . This composition operation closes the space of all operations  \mathcal{O}  under concatenation.

2.3 Value Functions and Future Value

The agent’s decision problem can be modelled as a semi‑Markov decision process (SMDP) [Puterman, 1994] where actions are either external actions (taken after stopping) or internal operations. The optimal state‑value function  V^* : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}  satisfies the Bellman optimality equation

V^*(s) = \max\Bigl\{\, U(s),\; \max_{o\in\mathcal{O}_0} \bigl( \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o) \bigr) \Bigr\},
\tag{1}

where

U(s) = \max_{a\in\mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}[R^e(x,a) \mid s]

is the value of stopping immediately and taking the best external action under the current internal state.

Future Value (FV). For any operation  o , its  Q ‑value and Future Value are

Q(s,o) = \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V^*(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o), \qquad
\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = Q(s,o) - U(s).
\tag{2}

An operation is worth executing in state  s  iff  \mathrm{FV}(o; s) > 0 . The optimal meta‑policy selects an operation with maximal positive FV; if none exists, it stops.

