3. Necessity of FV‑Greedy Selection

We establish that FV‑maximization is not a heuristic but a necessary property of any optimal policy in the SMDP.

Lemma 1 (Contraction and unique  V^* ).
Define the operator  T: C_b(\mathcal{S}) \to C_b(\mathcal{S})  by

(T V)(s) = \max\Bigl\{ U(s),\; \max_{o\in\mathcal{O}_0} \bigl( \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V(s') \mid s,o] - c(s,o) \bigr) \Bigr\}.

Since  \tau(s,o) \ge 1  almost surely for all  s,o , we have  \|T V_1 - T V_2\|_\infty \le \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty . By the Banach fixed‑point theorem,  T  possesses a unique fixed point  V^* \in C_b(\mathcal{S}) . This  V^*  is the optimal state‑value function.

Proof. Standard: the factor  \gamma^{\tau} \le \gamma  because  \tau \ge 1 , so the expectation over  V  is a  \gamma -contraction, and the max preserves contraction. ∎

Theorem 1 (Necessity of FV‑greedy meta‑policy).
Any policy  \pi  attaining  V^\pi(s) = V^*(s)  for all  s  must, at every state visited with positive probability, select an action (stop or an internal operation) that is FV‑greedy, i.e.,

\pi(s) \in \arg\max\bigl\{0,\; \max_{o} \mathrm{FV}(o; s) \bigr\}.

Proof.
Sufficiency: If a policy is FV‑greedy everywhere, then its value function satisfies (1) and thus equals  V^*  by uniqueness.
Necessity: Suppose there exists a state  s_0  with positive occupancy under  \pi  where  \pi  selects an operation  o  such that  \mathrm{FV}(o; s_0) < \max\{0, \max_{o'} \mathrm{FV}(o'; s_0)\} . The one‑step deviation principle for SMDPs [Puterman, 1994, Thm. 6.2.10] guarantees that switching to the FV‑greedy choice at  s_0  strictly improves the value at  s_0 , contradicting optimality. Hence  \pi  must be FV‑greedy. ∎

Thus every bounded‑optimal agent must internally evaluate operations by their Future Value.

