4. Composition and the Regret Decomposition

A central question is how the value of a sequence of operations relates to the values of its components. We show that FV does not simply add, but obeys a regret decomposition.

Definition 1 (Instantaneous Regret).
For any operation  o  and state  s ,

\mathrm{Regret}(o; s) := V^*(s) - Q(s,o) \ge 0.

Equivalently,  \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = [V^*(s) - U(s)] - \mathrm{Regret}(o; s) .

Theorem 2 (Regret decomposition for composed operations).
Let  \mathbf{o} = (o_1, \dots, o_k)  be a sequence of operations. Denote  S_0 = s ,  S_i \sim T_{o_i}(\cdot \mid S_{i-1}) , and let the cumulative discounted time be  D_{i-1} = \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \tau(S_{j-1}, o_j)  with  D_0 = 0 . Then

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = \bigl[V^*(s) - U(s)\bigr] \;-\; \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}\!\Bigl[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \, \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \;\Big|\; s \Bigr].
\tag{3}

Proof. We proceed by induction on  k .
Base case  k=1 : By definition  \mathrm{FV}(o_1; s) = [V^*(s) - U(s)] - \mathrm{Regret}(o_1; s) .
Inductive step: Assume (3) holds for all sequences of length  k-1 . For the  k -step sequence, we separate the first operation:

Q\bigl(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})\bigr) = \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{\tau_1} Q\bigl(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)\bigr) \Bigm| s, o_1 \Bigr] - c(s, o_1),

where  \tau_1 = \tau(s, o_1) . Applying the induction hypothesis to the tail,

Q(S_1, \mathcal{C}(o_2,\dots,o_k)) = V^*(S_1) - \sum_{i=2}^k \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{D'_{i-1}} \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \Bigm| S_1 \Bigr],

with  D'_{i-1} = \sum_{j=2}^{i} \tau(S_{j-1}, o_j) . Substituting back and noting  \gamma^{\tau_1}\gamma^{D'_{i-1}} = \gamma^{D_{i-1}} , we obtain

Q(s, \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o})) = Q(s, o_1) - \sum_{i=2}^k \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \Bigm| s \Bigr].

Using  Q(s, o_1) = V^*(s) - \mathrm{Regret}(o_1; s)  and subtracting  U(s)  yields (3). ∎

Corollary 2a (Optimal sequences are trivial).
If each  o_i  is FV‑optimal in the state it is applied (i.e.,  \mathrm{Regret}_i = 0  a.s.), then  \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = V^*(s) - U(s) . Hence a sequence of individually optimal steps offers no extra benefit over stopping after the first optimal operation.

Corollary 2b (Error propagation under suboptimal choices).
If  \mathrm{Regret}(o_i; S_{i-1}) \le \varepsilon  almost surely for all  i , then

\mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) \ge [V^*(s) - U(s)] - \varepsilon \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{D_{i-1}} \mid s] \ge [V^*(s) - U(s)] - k\varepsilon .

This bound is central to controlling the effect of estimation errors in the meta‑cognitive algorithm.

