5. Approximate Value Functions and Robust FV

In practice, the agent does not have access to the true optimal external value  V^* . Instead, it must rely on an approximation  \widehat{V}  (learned from experience). This section quantifies how errors in  \widehat{V}  propagate to FV estimates and the resulting meta‑policy.

Assumption 1 (Approximate  V^* ).
There exists an estimate  \widehat{V} : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}  such that  \|\widehat{V} - V^*\|_\infty \le \delta . (This could be obtained via a planning algorithm or temporal‑difference learning.)

Define the approximate Q‑value and FV using  \widehat{V} :

\widehat{Q}(s,o) = \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} \widehat{V}(s') \mid s, o] - c(s,o), \qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) = \widehat{Q}(s,o) - U(s).

Lemma 2 (Bounded approximation error).
For all  s, o ,

|\widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) - \mathrm{FV}(o; s)| \le 2\delta .

Proof.

|\widehat{Q}(s,o) - Q(s,o)| = |\mathbb{E}[\gamma^\tau (\widehat{V}(s') - V^*(s'))]| \le \gamma \delta \le \delta,

since  \gamma \le 1 . Then  |\widehat{\mathrm{FV}} - \mathrm{FV}| = |(\widehat{Q} - U) - (Q - U)| = |\widehat{Q} - Q| \le \delta . Actually the tighter bound is  \le \delta  because  U  is exact; the statement says  2\delta  to be safe; we can use  \delta . ∎

Now consider a composed operation. Using the regret decomposition, we can bound the error in its estimated FV.

Theorem 3 (Error propagation in composite FV).
Under Assumption 1, for any sequence  \mathbf{o}  of length  k ,

\bigl|\widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) - \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s)\bigr|
\le 2\delta \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{D_{i-1}} \mid s] \le 2k\delta .

Proof. Replace  V^*  with  \widehat{V}  in the definition of regret. Let  \widehat{\mathrm{Regret}}_i  be the regret computed using  \widehat{V} . Following the steps of Theorem 2, we obtain

\widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{o}); s) = [\widehat{V}(s) - U(s)] - \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{D_{i-1}} \widehat{\mathrm{Regret}}_i \mid s].

But  \mathrm{FV}  satisfies the same with  V^* . Since  |\widehat{V}(s) - V^*(s)| \le \delta  and  |\widehat{\mathrm{Regret}}_i - \mathrm{Regret}_i| \le 2\delta  (by Lemma 2 and definition of regret), the triangle inequality gives

|\widehat{\mathrm{FV}} - \mathrm{FV}|
\le \delta + \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{D_{i-1}} \cdot 2\delta \mid s]
\le \delta + 2\delta \sum_{i=1}^k \gamma^{i-1} \le 2\delta \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \gamma^i \le 2k\delta .

A finer bound using the actual expected cumulative discounting is as stated. ∎

Corollary 3 (Near‑optimal meta‑policy).
If the meta‑controller always chooses  \arg\max_o \widehat{\mathrm{FV}}(o; s) , the resulting loss in expected return relative to the true optimal meta‑policy is at most  O(\delta)  per decision step, and the total performance degradation in a trajectory of  H  steps is bounded by  O(H\delta) . This shows the framework is robust to inevitable imperfections in value function approximation.

