6. Hierarchical Emergence of Irreducible Operations

We now prove that composed operations can exhibit genuinely novel FV profiles—equivalence classes not realizable by any shallower composition—and that under generic conditions this leads to an unbounded growth in cognitive repertoire.

Definition 2 (Operation equivalence).
Two operations  o, o'  are equivalent,  o \sim o' , if  \mathrm{FV}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o'; s)  for all  s \in \mathcal{S} . Let  [o]  denote the equivalence class and  \Gamma(\mathcal{O})  the set of equivalence classes.

Composition hierarchy.
 \mathcal{O}_0  is the finite set of primitives. For  k \ge 0 ,

\mathcal{O}_{k+1} = \mathcal{O}_k \;\cup\; \bigl\{ \mathcal{C}(o_1, \dots, o_m) : o_i \in \mathcal{O}_k,\; 1 \le m \le K \bigr\},

with  K \ge 2  (typically  K=2  for binary composition).

6.1 Hankel Matrix and Non‑Degeneracy

Fix a set of probe states  P_k \subset \mathcal{S}  with  |P_k| = n^k . The Hankel matrix of depth  k  is

H_k \in \mathbb{R}^{P_k \times \mathcal{O}_0^k}, \qquad (H_k)_{p,w} = \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(w); p),

where each word  w = o_{i_1}\dots o_{i_k} \in \mathcal{O}_0^k  denotes the composed operation.

Axiom (Non‑Degeneracy).
For every  k \ge 1 , the matrix  H_k  has full column rank  n^k .

Full column rank means the  n^k  FV functions corresponding to length‑ k  sequences are linearly independent when restricted to the probe states. This is the natural requirement that distinct sequences generically produce distinct valuation profiles.

Connection to predictive state representations (PSRs).
In PSR theory, the state of a dynamical system is summarized by a vector of predictions of future observations [Littman et al., 2001]. A system is observable if the infinite Hankel matrix of successive action-observation sequences has finite rank, and the rank equals the dimension of the minimal sufficient statistic. Here, the FV functions act as “future expected values” under different operation sequences. The non‑degeneracy condition asserts that the family of length‑ k  operation sequences is rich enough to span a space of dimension  n^k ; in other words, the internal cognitive process is sufficiently expressive that history length  k  yields linearly independent value profiles. This is analogous to the condition that a PSR has full rank up to depth  k , and it guarantees that no shallower composition can mimic a deeper one.

6.2 Genericity of the Non‑Degeneracy Condition

We prove that the full‑rank condition is generic in any smoothly parametrised environment.

Proposition 1 (Genericity).
Assume the environment’s external transition kernel  P^e , reward  R^e , the primitive operation kernels  T_{a} , and costs  c(\cdot, a)  depend real‑analytically on a parameter  \theta  from a connected open set  \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d . Then the set of parameters for which any  H_k  fails to have full column rank is a closed subset of  \Theta  of Lebesgue measure zero.

Proof sketch. The Bellman operator  T_\theta  in (1) is a uniform  \gamma -contraction whose components depend analytically on  \theta . Hence its unique fixed point  V^*_\theta  is a real‑analytic function of  \theta  (for finite state spaces this follows from the analytic implicit function theorem; for Polish spaces similar regularity holds under mild conditions). Every entry of  H_k(\theta)  is a composition of  V^*_\theta , expectations, and costs, and is therefore real‑analytic. The determinant of any  n^k \times n^k  square submatrix of  H_k  is real‑analytic. By the identity theorem for real‑analytic functions, such a determinant is either identically zero on  \Theta  or vanishes only on a closed, measure‑zero subset.

To show the determinant cannot be identically zero, we construct a witness parameter  \theta_0 . Choose an internal state space that contains a perfect memory register recording the last  k  operations as a distinct symbol. Let the terminal utility  U(s)  be an injective function of this memory. Then each length‑ k  sequence produces a different final state and a different FV on a suitably chosen set of probe states, ensuring full rank. Because the parameterised family is rich enough to contain such a configuration (e.g., by letting the memory kernels depend on a parameter that interpolates to this perfect memory), the determinant is not identically zero. Hence, full rank holds for almost all  \theta . ∎

6.3 Unbounded Emergence

Theorem 4 (Unbounded emergence).
Under the Non‑Degeneracy Axiom, for every  k \ge 1 ,

|\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k,

and consequently  \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) \setminus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_{k-1}) \neq \emptyset . The number of distinct equivalence classes grows without bound as  k \to \infty .

Proof.
The hierarchy  \mathcal{O}_k  contains all length‑ k  sequences of primitives. By the axiom, the  n^k  columns of  H_k  are distinct, so the corresponding composed operations belong to  n^k  distinct equivalence classes. Hence  |\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k . Since  |\mathcal{O}_{k-1}| \le \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} n^i = (n^k-1)/(n-1) < n^k  for  n \ge 2 , the pigeonhole principle guarantees the existence of new classes at depth  k . ∎

The theorem formalizes the intuition that an FV‑maximising agent endowed with even a small set of primitives will inevitably generate an unbounded repertoire of genuinely novel cognitive operations, provided the environment is generic. No external designer needs to specify these compositions; they emerge autonomously from the normative pressure to maximise FV.

