8. Unification of Perception, Reasoning, and Memory

The framework unifies three previously separate cognitive functions under FV maximization.

Perception (decision‑centric rate‑distortion).
A perception operation  o_{\text{perc}}  acquires an observation  y  and updates the internal state to  s \oplus y . Encoding cost is  \lambda I(X;Y \mid s)  (in reward units), duration  \tau = 1 . Then

\mathrm{FV}(o_{\text{perc}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}_y[V^*(s \oplus y)] - U(s) - \lambda I(X;Y \mid s),

exactly the decision‑centric rate‑distortion objective.

Reasoning (Future Reasoning Value).
A reasoning step  o_{\text{reason}}  applies a neural computation (e.g., a transformer layer) resulting in state  s'  with cost  c_{\text{FLOPs}}  and duration  \tau = 1 . Its FV is

\mathrm{FRV}(o_{\text{reason}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s] - U(s) - c_{\text{FLOPs}}.

Optimal stopping occurs when  \max_o \mathrm{FRV} \le 0 .

Memory (Future Memory Value).
Storing an item  m  incurs cost  c_{\text{store}}  and duration  1 , yielding new state  s \cup \{m\} . Thus

\mathrm{FMV}(o_{\text{mem}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V^*(s \cup \{m\})] - U(s) - c_{\text{store}}.

Only memories with positive FMV are admitted.

Cross‑modal sequences. Because all operations are evaluated in the common FV currency, sequences such as “perceive then reason” are simply composed operations whose value is given by the regret decomposition (3). The framework does not promise automatic super‑additivity; rather, it allows the agent to discover beneficial sequences when the environment makes them worthwhile. Under the non‑degeneracy condition, many such sequences yield genuinely new cognitive behaviors.

