9. Discussion and Future Work

The revised theory provides a rigorous normative foundation for meta‑level cognitive control. Yet several open challenges remain:

· Tractable discovery of deep compositions. The exponential growth of the Hankel rank suggests that exhaustive enumeration is impossible; future work should integrate intrinsic motivation (curiosity) and learning progress signals to guide composition proposal toward promising regions.
· Model‑free approximations. The current analysis assumes a (possibly learned) world model for FV rollouts. Extending the regret decomposition to model‑free TD‑learning of composite operations would bring the theory closer to practical deep RL agents.
· Alignment and safety. As the agent autonomously invents new operations, verifying that they remain safe and aligned with human values becomes critical. The primitive‑based construction provides a natural bottleneck: if all primitives are safe, any composition is syntactically safe. However, semantic safety (the effects of new sequences) requires formal verification, which we leave for future work.
· Empirical validation. Implementing the meta‑cognitive algorithm in a challenging environment (e.g., a complex reasoning or robotics domain) would test whether the emergence of novel cognitive behaviors is observable and beneficial.
· Connections to human cognition. The chunking‑based explanation of why agents pre‑compile sequences mirrors psychological findings; further experimental work could test whether human cognitive control obeys an FV‑maximization principle.

