B. Necessity of FV‑Greedy Selection

Definition B.1 (Future Value).
For any operation o and state s,

Q(s,o) = \mathbb{E}[ \gamma^{\tau(s,o)} V^*(s') \mid s, o ] - c(s,o), \qquad
\mathrm{FV}(o; s) = Q(s,o) - U(s).

Definition B.2 (FV‑greedy policy).
A stationary policy \pi^* is FV‑greedy if for every state s,

\pi^*(s) \in \arg\max\Bigl\{0,\; \max_{o \in \mathcal{O}_0} \mathrm{FV}(o; s) \Bigr\},

with the convention \mathrm{FV}(\bot; s) = 0 for the stop action \bot.

Theorem B.1 (Necessity and sufficiency of FV‑greediness).
A stationary policy \pi satisfies V^\pi \equiv V^* if and only if \pi is FV‑greedy.

Proof.
Sufficiency: If \pi^* is FV‑greedy, then its value function satisfies V^{\pi^*} = T V^{\pi^*} by construction. Since T has a unique fixed point V^* (Theorem A.2), it follows that V^{\pi^*} = V^*.

Necessity: Suppose V^\pi \equiv V^* but \pi is not FV‑greedy. Then there exists a state s_0 with positive occupancy under \pi where \pi selects an action (some o_0 or \bot) that does not achieve the maximum FV. Hence there exists an alternative choice a^* (an operation or stop) with \mathrm{FV}(a^*; s_0) > \mathrm{FV}(\pi(s_0); s_0). Let \pi' be the policy that follows \pi everywhere except at the first visit to s_0 it takes a^* and thereafter reverts to \pi. By the principle of optimality for SMDPs (one‑step deviation; Puterman, 1994, Thm. 6.2.10),

V^{\pi'}(s_0) - V^{\pi}(s_0) = Q(s_0, a^*) - Q(s_0, \pi(s_0)) = \mathrm{FV}(a^*; s_0) - \mathrm{FV}(\pi(s_0); s_0) > 0,

contradicting the optimality of V^* = V^\pi. Therefore \pi must be FV‑greedy. ∎

