D. Hierarchical Emergence of Irreducible Operations

We provide rigorous statements and proofs for the emergence results, including the genericity of the Hankel full‑rank condition and a weak alternative.

D.1 Hankel Matrix and Non‑Degeneracy

Fix a finite set of probe states P_k \subset \mathcal{S} with |P_k| = n^k. The Hankel matrix of depth k is

H_k \in \mathbb{R}^{P_k \times \mathcal{O}_0^k}, \qquad (H_k)_{p,w} = \mathrm{FV}(\mathcal{C}(w); p),

where each word w = o_{i_1}\dots o_{i_k} \in \mathcal{O}_0^k denotes the composed operation.

Axiom D.1 (Non‑Degeneracy).
For every k \ge 1, the matrix H_k has full column rank n^k.

Full column rank means that the n^k FV functions corresponding to length‑k sequences, when restricted to the probe states, are linearly independent.

D.2 Genericity of the Non‑Degeneracy Condition

Proposition D.2 (Genericity).
Assume the environment’s external transition kernel P^e, reward R^e, the primitive operation kernels T_{a}, and costs c(\cdot, a) depend real‑analytically on a parameter \theta from a connected open set \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d. Then the set of parameters for which any H_k fails to have full column rank is a closed subset of \Theta of Lebesgue measure zero.

Proof. We construct the proof for a finite state space \mathcal{S}; the extension to Polish spaces holds under mild regularity conditions (e.g., uniform convergence of analytic approximants) but the core idea is identical.

Step 1: Analyticity of V^*_\theta.
For each fixed \theta, the operator T_\theta defined by (A.1) is a \gamma-contraction on \mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|} equipped with the max‑norm. Its components (transition probabilities, costs, rewards) are real‑analytic functions of \theta. The fixed point V^*_\theta satisfies V^*_\theta = T_\theta V^*_\theta. Consider the function F(\theta, V) = V - T_\theta V. F is real‑analytic in (\theta, V) and its partial derivative with respect to V is I - D_V T_\theta, where D_V T_\theta is a contraction with norm \le \gamma < 1. Hence the implicit function theorem for real‑analytic functions (or the Banach‑space version) guarantees that the unique solution V^*_\theta is real‑analytic in \theta. (See e.g., Deimling, Nonlinear Functional Analysis, §15.)

Step 2: Analyticity of H_k(\theta).
Each entry \mathrm{FV}_\theta(\mathcal{C}(w); p) is computed by simulating the operation sequence from p, applying the corresponding transition kernels, costs, and evaluating V^*_\theta at the final state, then subtracting U_\theta(p). All these components are real‑analytic in \theta (the kernels, costs, and U_\theta are analytic by assumption; V^*_\theta is analytic by Step 1; composition of real‑analytic functions is real‑analytic). Thus H_k(\theta) is a matrix of real‑analytic functions.

Step 3: Determinant and identity theorem.
Consider any n^k \times n^k square submatrix of H_k, obtained by selecting a subset of the rows (probe states) of size n^k. Its determinant \Delta(\theta) is a real‑analytic function of \theta. By the identity theorem for real‑analytic functions, \Delta(\theta) is either identically zero on \Theta or vanishes only on a closed set of Lebesgue measure zero (the set of its zeros is a proper real‑analytic subvariety). The union over all such submatrices is also a null set.

Step 4: Witness parameter.
We must show that at least one such determinant is not identically zero. Construct a witness \theta_0 as follows. Let the internal state contain a perfect memory register of the last k operations. Specifically, let \mathcal{S} be large enough to include states labeled by the sequence of the last k primitive operations. Define the kernels such that applying operation o appends o to this memory, discarding the oldest entry to keep length k. The cost and duration can be set to trivial values (e.g., zero cost and unit duration). Choose the terminal utility U(s) to be an injective function of the memory contents, e.g., assign distinct real numbers to each of the n^k possible length‑k sequences. Then for any probe state p that initializes a distinct memory, the FV of a sequence w will be essentially U of the final memory after executing w. With proper alignment of probe states and words, the Hankel matrix becomes a permutation matrix (or a diagonal matrix), which has full rank. Because the parameterised family is assumed to be rich enough to interpolate to this witness (e.g., by continuously varying a “memory sharpness” parameter), the determinant cannot be identically zero on the connected open set \Theta. Hence it is non‑zero almost everywhere. ∎

D.3 Unbounded Emergence

Theorem D.3 (Unbounded emergence).
Under Axiom D.1, for every k \ge 1,

|\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k,

and consequently \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k) \setminus \Gamma(\mathcal{O}_{k-1}) \neq \emptyset. The number of distinct equivalence classes grows without bound as k \to \infty.

Proof.
The hierarchy \mathcal{O}_k contains all length‑k sequences of primitives (by iterated pairwise composition). By the axiom, the n^k columns of H_k are distinct, so the corresponding composed operations belong to n^k distinct equivalence classes. Therefore |\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| \ge n^k. Since |\mathcal{O}_{k-1}| \le \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} n^i = (n^k - 1)/(n-1) < n^k for n \ge 2, the pigeonhole principle guarantees new classes at depth k. ∎

D.4 Weak Emergence without Full Genericity

If the full‑rank axiom is considered too strong, a much weaker “contextual dependence” condition still yields unbounded emergence when coupled with a mild recurrence hypothesis.

Definition D.4 (Contextual dependence).
There exist primitives a,b \in \mathcal{O}_0 and a state s_0 such that

\Delta := \mathbb{E}[ \mathrm{FV}(b; S) \mid s_0, a] - \mathrm{FV}(b; s_0) > 0.

This says that applying a changes the FV of b, i.e., the value of a cognitive step depends on the context created by previous steps.

Theorem D.5 (Weak emergence under \varphi-irreducibility).
Suppose the state space is \varphi-irreducible under some policy: there exist m_0 \in \mathbb{N}, a sequence r \in \mathcal{O}_0^{m_0}, and \delta > 0 such that for every reachable state s,

\mathbb{P}(S_{m_0} = s_0 \mid S_0 = s, \text{sequence } r) \ge \delta.

Then for every k \ge 1 there exists an operation of depth at most (m_0+2)k whose FV equivalence class is not representable by any shallower operation. Hence \lim_{k\to\infty} |\Gamma(\mathcal{O}_k)| = \infty.

Proof sketch. Consider the composite operation w_k = (r, a, b)^k (repeat the block r,a,b k times). The block r resets to s_0 with probability at least \delta, after which a,b provide a synergistic gain \Delta. Using the regret decomposition (C.1), one can show that the FV of \mathcal{C}(w_k) exceeds the best value achievable by any composition of depth strictly less than k by at least c \cdot (\delta \gamma^{(m_0+2)})^k \Delta > 0 for some constant c, guaranteeing a new equivalence class at each multiple of m_0+2. A detailed expansion of the recurrence is straightforward but notationally heavy; the key step is that each new repetition of the block adds a positive contribution that cannot be mimicked by a static shallow policy. ∎

