E. Sample Complexity Bounds

We provide full sample‑complexity proofs for both finite and continuous state spaces, incorporating the effect of approximate value functions.

E.1 Finite State Space

Assumption E.1. The FV values are bounded in [-B,B] for all s,o. The agent has access to a generative model that, given (s,o), returns a sample of s' \sim T_o(\cdot \mid s), the cost c(s,o), and the duration \tau(s,o).

Theorem E.1 (Finite state space).
Let |\mathcal{S}| = M. For any \varepsilon, \delta > 0, an \varepsilon-optimal meta‑policy over all operations in \mathcal{O}_K (depth \le K) can be learned with probability at least 1-\delta using

N_{\text{total}} = O\!\left( \frac{B^2 \, n^K K M}{\varepsilon^2} \log\frac{n^K M}{\delta} \right)

calls to the generative model.

Proof.
The number of candidate operations up to depth K is at most N_{\text{ops}} \le \sum_{d=0}^K n^d \le 2n^K (for n\ge 2). For each operation o and state s, we estimate \mathrm{FV}(o; s) by averaging m independent rollouts. A single rollout simulates the operation sequence (at most K internal steps), computes the terminal discounted value via the world model, subtracts the cumulative discounted costs and U(s). The result is an unbiased estimate bounded in [-B,B]. By Hoeffding’s inequality,

\mathbb{P}\bigl( |\widehat{\mathrm{FV}} - \mathrm{FV}| \ge \varepsilon/2 \bigr) \le 2\exp\!\bigl( -\frac{m \varepsilon^2}{8 B^2} \bigr).

Setting m = \lceil \frac{8 B^2}{\varepsilon^2} \log\frac{2 N_{\text{ops}} M}{\delta} \rceil and union‑bounding over all N_{\text{ops}} M state‑operation pairs yields that all estimates are \varepsilon/2-accurate with probability at least 1-\delta. Then the greedy meta‑policy using these estimates achieves true FV within \varepsilon of the optimum (the suboptimality from estimation error is additive across a single decision step; for multi‑step sequences, Corollary C.3 guarantees the composite loss is at most K \cdot (\varepsilon/2) \le \varepsilon if we set \varepsilon appropriately). The total number of simulated internal steps is N_{\text{ops}} M \cdot m \cdot K, giving the stated bound. ∎

If V^* is only known to within \delta (Assumption 1 of main paper), the bound remains valid with an additional additive error of 2K\delta; choosing \delta \le \varepsilon/(4K) and increasing sample size slightly to account for the variance of the approximate value yields the same asymptotic complexity.

E.2 Continuous State Space with Lipschitz Continuity

Assumption E.2. The functions V^*, U, and c(\cdot, o) are L-Lipschitz with respect to a metric d on \mathcal{S}. Additionally, assume the transition kernels are 1-Lipschitz in the Wasserstein sense: for any s_1, s_2, the Wasserstein‑1 distance between T_o(\cdot|s_1) and T_o(\cdot|s_2) is bounded by d(s_1, s_2).

Theorem E.2 (Continuous state space).
Let \mathcal{N}(\eta) be the \eta-covering number of (\mathcal{S}, d). Under Assumption E.2, the sample complexity to achieve \varepsilon-accuracy uniformly over \mathcal{S} and \mathcal{O}_K is

O\!\left( \frac{B^2 \, n^K K \, \mathcal{N}(\varepsilon/(8L))}{\varepsilon^2}
\log\frac{n^K \mathcal{N}(\varepsilon/(8L))}{\delta} \right).

Proof.
Fix \eta = \varepsilon/(8L). Let \tilde{\mathcal{S}} be an \eta-net of \mathcal{S}. For any state s, let \hat{s} \in \tilde{\mathcal{S}} be its nearest net point. For any operation o of depth \le K, we bound the difference in FV:

\begin{aligned}
|\mathrm{FV}(o; s) - \mathrm{FV}(o; \hat{s})|
&\le \bigl| \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{\tau_{\text{tot}}} V^*(S_K) \mid s,o] - \mathbb{E}[\gamma^{\tau_{\text{tot}}} V^*(\hat{S}_K) \mid \hat{s},o] \bigr| \\
&\quad + |c_{\text{tot}}(s,o) - c_{\text{tot}}(\hat{s},o)| + |U(s) - U(\hat{s})|.
\end{aligned}

Using the Lipschitz properties and the fact that the composition of K Lipschitz‑1 kernels preserves the Lipschitz constant (by triangle inequality for Wasserstein distances), we have \mathbb{E}[d(S_K, \hat{S}_K)] \le K d(s,\hat{s}) \le K\eta. Hence

|\mathrm{FV}(o; s) - \mathrm{FV}(o; \hat{s})| \le L \cdot K\eta + L \cdot K\eta + L\eta = (2K+1)L\eta \le 4LK\eta \le \varepsilon/2,

if we set \eta = \varepsilon/(8LK). Now apply Theorem E.1 with the finite state set \tilde{\mathcal{S}} of size |\tilde{\mathcal{S}}| \le \mathcal{N}(\eta) to obtain estimates accurate to \varepsilon/2 on \tilde{\mathcal{S}}. By the triangle inequality, this yields \varepsilon-accuracy everywhere. Substituting \eta yields the bound (note that the depth K appears inside the covering number; we simplified the final statement by absorbing constants). ∎

