F. Unification of Perception, Reasoning, and Memory

We verify that the three previously separate theories are exact instantiations of the FV framework.

Perception (decision‑centric rate‑distortion).
A perception operation o_{\text{perc}} acquires a sensor observation y and updates the internal state to s' = s \oplus y. The encoding incurs an information‑theoretic cost c = \lambda I(X; Y \mid s) (in reward units) and takes duration \tau = 1. Then

\mathrm{FV}(o_{\text{perc}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}_y[V^*(s \oplus y)] - U(s) - \lambda I(X;Y \mid s),

which is exactly the objective of decision‑centric rate‑distortion theory.

Reasoning (Future Reasoning Value).
A reasoning step o_{\text{reason}} applies a neural computation (e.g., a transformer layer), resulting in a new state s' with cost c (FLOPs) and duration \tau = 1. Its FV is

\mathrm{FRV}(o_{\text{reason}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V^*(s') \mid s] - U(s) - c_{\text{FLOPs}}.

The optimal stopping rule is to cease reasoning when \max_o \mathrm{FRV} \le 0.

Memory (Future Memory Value).
Storing an item m costs c_{\text{store}} and takes one tick. The new state is s \cup \{m\}. Thus,

\mathrm{FMV}(o_{\text{mem}}; s) = \gamma \, \mathbb{E}[V^*(s \cup \{m\})] - U(s) - c_{\text{store}}.

Only memories with positive FMV are admitted.

Cross‑modal sequences. Because all operations are evaluated in the common FV currency, sequences such as “perceive then reason” are simply composed operations whose value is given by the regret decomposition (C.1). The framework does not promise automatic super‑additivity; rather, it allows the agent to discover beneficial sequences when the environment makes them worthwhile. Under the non‑degeneracy condition, many such sequences yield genuinely new cognitive behaviors.

