G. Meta‑Cognitive Algorithm: Chunking Incentive and Connection to Options

The algorithm of Section 7.1 can be augmented by an explicit chunking incentive that justifies why an agent would pre‑compile sequences even when each step appears optimal.

G.1 Effective FV with Meta‑Control Overhead

Assume that executing the meta‑controller (evaluating FV for the library and selecting an operation) itself costs  \kappa > 0 seconds of wall‑clock time, which translates to a discount factor \gamma^\kappa or an additive cost. If the agent executes a pre‑compiled macro o = \mathcal{C}(o_1,\dots,o_k) as a single operation, it pays this meta‑cost once. If instead it executed the sequence step‑by‑step with re‑optimisation after each step, it would pay the meta‑cost k times. The effective FV of a macro accounting for saved meta‑cost is

\mathrm{FV}_{\kappa}(o; s) = \mathrm{FV}(o; s) + (k-1)\kappa \quad (\text{under additive cost}).

Even if each individual step is optimal (\mathrm{Regret}=0), the sequence’s pure FV equals V^*(s)-U(s), but the effective FV is strictly larger due to the saved overhead. This provides a normative pressure to chunk operations, fully compatible with the chunking mechanisms in cognitive architectures like SOAR and ACT‑R.

G.2 Connection to the Options Framework

In the options framework (Sutton, Precup & Singh, 1999), an option is a triple (I, \pi, \beta) where I is an initiation set, \pi is a policy, and \beta is a termination condition. Here, a cognitive operation o (primitive or composite) can be seen as an option with:

· I = \mathcal{S} (always available unless pruned),
· \pi being the internal policy that executes the sequence of primitives, and
· \beta(s) = 1 after the fixed number of steps (or when the sequence ends) and 0 otherwise.

The meta‑controller learns the value Q_{\Omega}(s, o) (the option value) and selects among options. Composition of primitives into longer options corresponds exactly to the creation of new options. The theory thus provides a normative rationale for autonomous option discovery grounded in FV maximization.

