H. Extensions: Approximate Models and Safety

H.1 Model‑Free Learning of Composite FV

If a world model is not available, one can learn the Q‑value of composite operations directly from environment interaction using temporal‑difference (TD) learning. Consider a parameterised value function \widehat{Q}_\phi(o, s). After executing operation o and observing the resulting sequence of rewards and costs, the TD error can be defined as

\delta = \sum_{t=0}^{\tau_{\text{tot}}-1} \gamma^t r_{t} - c_{\text{tot}} + \gamma^{\tau_{\text{tot}}} \max_{o'} \widehat{Q}_\phi(o', s_{\text{end}}) - \widehat{Q}_\phi(o, s),

where r_t are external rewards received during the operation’s execution (if the environment does not pause) and c_{\text{tot}} is the total computational cost. The regret decomposition still applies to the true value, and the error‑propagation bounds (Theorem 3) can be adapted to the bias introduced by function approximation.

H.2 Safety and Alignment

Because all composite operations are built from primitives, any property that is preserved by composition will hold for all generated operations. For instance, if every primitive is certified safe (e.g., does not modify certain protected memory regions), then any sequence of them is syntactically safe. Semantic safety—that the overall behavior stays within acceptable bounds—can be monitored by the FV signal itself: if an operation’s upper confidence bound on FV is negative, it is pruned. One can also assign prohibitive costs to primitives that lead to undesirable states, which will propagate to any composition containing them via the regret decomposition. Formal verification of safety under the FV‑maximization regime is a promising direction for future work.

